%T A402446 7,20,21,22,23,24,25,8,26,4,27,2,9,28,29,10,30,31,11,5,32,33,34,35,12,
%U A402446 36,3,1,37,38,39,13,40,41,42,43,6,14,44,45,15,46,47,7,16,48,49,17,50,51,52,53,54
%N A402446 Card read at step n if, at each step, the top card of the deck 1, 2, 3, ... is read, removed and put back below 2*(n - k) of the remaining cards, where k is the last earlier step at which this card was read (k = 0 if there is none).
%C A402446 The deck is infinite and starts as 1, 2, 3, ... with card 1 on top. Call n - k the age of the card read at step n (n if it was not read before), and the number of cards above it after it is put back its depth: here the depth is twice the age, while in A402125 it equals the age.
%C A402446 When a card is put back, all cards with smaller labels lie above it. Hence, for each r >= 1, the cards are read for the r-th time in increasing order of their labels, and the number K(t) of distinct terms among a(1), ..., a(t) equals max(a(1), ..., a(t)) (proved in the notes file).
%C A402446 Every card is read infinitely often. From some reading on, each gap between consecutive readings of a card is exactly twice the previous gap plus 1, the least the rule allows, so a fixed card is read log_2(t) + O(1) times among the first t steps (proved in the notes file; see the Formula section). The reading from which this holds tends to infinity with the label of the card (computer-assisted proof in the notes file).
%C A402446 K(t)/t tends to alpha = 0.63625139817510655785... (A402447). More generally, let C_r(t) be the number of cards read at least r times in the first t steps; then C_r(t)/t tends to b_r, where b_1 = alpha and b_(r+1) = b_r*(b_1 + ... + b_r)/2, and the b_r add up to 1 (computer-assisted proofs in the notes file). If step n is an r-th reading, then a(n) = C_r(n), so a(n)/n tends to b_r along these steps, and in the plot the r-th readings lie near the line of slope b_r through the origin.
%C A402446 For real x >= 0 let H_0(x) = 0, H_1(x) = x, H_(r+1)(x) = H_r(x)*(1 + H_r(x) - H_(r-1)(x)) for r >= 1, and H(x) = lim_{r->oo} H_r(x), which may be infinite. Since H_r(alpha/2) = (b_1 + ... + b_r)/2 and the b_r add up to 1, alpha/2 is the only x > 0 with H(x) = 1/2 (computer-assisted proof in the notes file). The coefficients of H_r(x) converge to A045761 and H_r(1) = A001696(r) (proved in the notes file). In A402125 the only possible limit of K(t)/t is the x > 0 with H(x) = 1 (A268107), but its convergence is a conjecture there.
%C A402446 Putting the top card back below twice its label gives A104706, which agrees with a(n) for n <= 6 but not for n = 7. Putting the card read at step n back below 2*n cards gives A253887(n+1) as the card read at step n (proved in the notes file), which agrees with a(n) for n <= 10 but not for n = 11. Decks in which the top card goes back to a prescribed depth were studied before: by Darnell and Dribus (the depth is the label) and in the flashcard games of Novikoff, Kleinberg, and Strogatz and of Lewis and Li (the depth depends on the number of readings). See the notes file in the Links section for proofs, sources and further data.
